## 4. Privacy Regulations — Key Recommendations

### 4.1 Consent by Age

| Age Group | Consent Required From | Type |
|---|---|---|
| Under 16 | Parent/guardian + adolescent assent | Written informed consent |
| 16–17 | Parent/guardian + adolescent | Written informed consent |
| 18+ | Individual only | Written informed consent |

### 4.2 Informed Consent Must Cover

1. What data is collected and how
2. How it is processed (NLP analysis — not human reading of messages)
3. What outputs are generated (risk scores, not diagnoses)
4. Who sees the outputs (counsellors only, not teachers or parents unless escalation)
5. What actions may follow (counsellor outreach — not automatic referral)
6. Right to withdraw at any time without consequence
7. Data retention schedule and deletion process
8. Limitations of the system — it can miss cases; it is not a diagnosis
9. How to make a complaint or access stored data

### 4.3 Privacy Recommendations by Jurisdiction

| Jurisdiction | Key Additional Requirement |
|---|---|
| Singapore | Notify PDPC within 3 days of data breach involving significant harm |
| EU | Appoint Data Protection Officer; conduct DPIA before deployment |
| USA | HIPAA Business Associate Agreement with any third-party processors |
| China | Data localisation — personal data of Chinese citizens cannot leave China |
| Japan | Explicit consent required before providing sensitive data to third parties |

